# Course-eligibility relational reasoning (real-inference demo).
#
# A genuinely different task from examples/attn_multihead_retrieval.tns's
# Associative Recall (AR): AR's answer is a fact stated directly in the
# sequence (key -> value, verbatim). This task's answer is never
# stated. The input describes (a) up to 3 courses the student has
# already completed, and (b) each of 6 candidate courses' single
# prerequisite -- but nowhere does it say "the student is eligible for
# X". Example, written in plain words (the model sees only
# embeddings):
#
#     Completed: CS101, MA101
#     Prerequisites: CS201 needs CS101 | MA201 needs MA101
#                    | PHY101 needs (none) | BIO101 needs (none)
#     Eligible (not completed, prerequisite satisfied): PHY101
#
# Solving it needs two chained lookups, not one: attend over the
# completed-course facts to build a "profile" of what the student
# knows (hop 1), then, separately for each candidate course, check
# whether *that* course's prerequisite is covered by the profile, and
# whether the course itself is already done (hop 2) -- exactly the
# "attend to completed courses, attend separately to requirements,
# combine, ignore what doesn't apply" reasoning a lookup table cannot
# do. tools/models/prepare_relational_demo.py trains this once in
# PyTorch; a feasibility spike found this reliably reaches ~75-80%
# held-out accuracy (see that script's module docstring) -- clearly
# above the 1/6 ~ 17% chance rate, confirming genuine two-hop
# fact-chaining, but, honestly, well short of AR's ~100%, since this
# is a strictly harder task. The comparison below is still exact
# (Tensora vs. this same imperfect PyTorch model), same as every other
# real-inference demo; only the *task* is harder, not the tolerance.
#
# Hop 1 is single-head softmax attention, same Q@Kt / softmax / A@V
# shape as attn_multihead_retrieval.tns, but the query is a fixed
# *learned* "profile query" (Wq1) rather than a per-example token --
# there is nothing to look up yet, just a fixed question ("what has
# this student completed?") asked of the completion facts. The
# null-padding completion slots (fewer than 3 courses completed) are
# never masked by hand -- v1 Tensora has no masking op either -- the
# attention weights simply learn to give them low score.
#
# Hop 2 has no attention op at all: `profile` is projected two ways
# (Wcov = "does this cover a course's prerequisite?", Walr = "is this
# course itself already done?") and matmul'd against every candidate
# course's prerequisite embedding (Xpp) and its own embedding (Xpc),
# giving one `coverage` and one `already` score per course. The two
# per-course score tensors are combined with two learned scalars
# (Wa, Wb) and a bias -- plain elementwise `*`/`+`, since v1 Tensora
# has no stack/concat op to feed a combining MLP instead.
#
# The trailing `sq=1` axis (hop 1's attention query has exactly one
# row -- there is only one "profile" per example) is dropped with
# `sum(..., axis=sq)`, the same no-indexing "squeeze a singleton axis"
# trick attn_multihead_retrieval.tns uses for `axis=seq_q`.
#
# Scale = 1/sqrt(dk) = 1/sqrt(32); the prep script uses the same
# constant so the reference matches.
#
# The batch axis b is 200 (larger than AR's 30): this task's accuracy
# is well under 100%, so a bigger held-out batch is needed for the
# printed accuracy number to be statistically meaningful rather than
# noise from a handful of examples.
input  Xcomp: Tensor[b=200, ncomp=3, d=32]
input  Xpp:   Tensor[b=200, courses=6, d=32]
input  Xpc:   Tensor[b=200, courses=6, d=32]
weight Wk1:  Tensor[b=1, d=32, dk=32]
weight Wv1:  Tensor[b=1, d=32, dv=32]
weight Wq1:  Tensor[b=1, sq=1, dk=32]
weight Wcov: Tensor[b=1, dv=32, dc=32]
weight Walr: Tensor[b=1, dv=32, dc=32]
weight Wa:   Tensor[b=1, sq=1, courses=1]
weight Wb:   Tensor[b=1, sq=1, courses=1]
weight Bias: Tensor[b=1, sq=1, courses=1]

graph attn_relational {
    K1       = Xcomp @ Wk1
    V1       = Xcomp @ Wv1
    K1t      = transpose(K1, perm=[0, 2, 1])
    scores1  = Wq1 @ K1t
    scaled1  = scores1 * 0.17677670
    A1       = softmax(scaled1, axis=ncomp)
    profile  = A1 @ V1
    coverQ   = profile @ Wcov
    alreadyQ = profile @ Walr
    XppT     = transpose(Xpp, perm=[0, 2, 1])
    XpcT     = transpose(Xpc, perm=[0, 2, 1])
    coverage = coverQ @ XppT
    already  = alreadyQ @ XpcT
    covered  = coverage * Wa
    covdone  = already * Wb
    summed   = covered + covdone
    combined = summed + Bias
    logits   = sum(combined, axis=sq)
    return logits
}
